WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.720
We know why kids don't learn.&nbsp;
It's either poverty,
&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.920
low attendance, negative peer influences—
we know why.
&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.320
But one of the things that we never&nbsp;
discuss or we rarely discuss
&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.400
is the value and importance&nbsp;
of human connection.
&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.920
Relationships.
I have had classes that were so low,
&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.760
so academically deficient, that I cried. 
&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.560
I wondered, "How am I going to take&nbsp;
this group, in nine months,
&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.160
from where they are to where they need to be?
How do I raise the self-esteem of a child
&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.400
and his academic achievement&nbsp;
at the same time?"
&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.640
One year I came up with a bright&nbsp;
idea. I told all my students, 
&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.800
"You were chosen to be in my class
because I am the best teacher and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.600
are the best students. 
They put us all together so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.760
could show everybody else
how to do it."
&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.560
One of the students said, "Really?"
I said, "Really."
&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.880
And I gave them a saying to say:
"I am somebody. I was somebody when I came.
&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.120
I'll be a better somebody when I leave.
I am powerful, and I am strong. 
&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.000
I deserve the education that I get here.
I have things to do, people to impress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.480
and places to go."
And they said, "Yeah!"
&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.560
You say it long enough, it&nbsp;
starts to be a part of you.
&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.880
Both my parents were educators.
My maternal grandparents were educators.
&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.920
For years, I watched my mother take&nbsp;
the time at recess to review,
&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.600
go on home visits in the afternoon, 
buy combs and brushes and peanut butter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.960
and crackers 
to put in her desk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.960
drawer for kids that needed to eat, 
and a washcloth and some soap for the kids
&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.760
who didn't smell so good. See, it's&nbsp;
hard to teach kids who stink.
&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.640
Years later, after she retired,
I watched some of those same kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.920
come through and say to her,
"You know, Ms. Walker, you made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.120
a difference in my life.
You made it work for me.
&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.000
You made me feel like I was somebody,
when I knew, at the bottom, I wasn't.
&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.360
And I want you to just see what I've become."
And when my mama died two years ago at 92,
&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.320
there were so many former&nbsp;
students at her funeral,
&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.800
it brought tears to my eyes,&nbsp;
not because she was gone,
&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.640
but because she left a&nbsp;
legacy of relationships
&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.560
that could never disappear.
Every child deserves a champion, 
&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.680
an adult who will never give up on them,
who understands the power of connection, 
&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.040
and insists that they become the&nbsp;
best that they can possibly be.
&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.600
Is this job tough? You betcha.&nbsp;
Oh God, you betcha.
&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.960
But it is not impossible. We can do this.
We're educators. We're born to make a difference.

